" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK/main/README.md)\nciteturn40view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn33view0\",\"lineno\":1}); Total lines: 253\nL0: <p align=\"center\">\nL1:   <img src=\"examples/images/logo.png\" alt=\"GEAK v4\" width=\"300\">\nL2: </p>\nL3: \nL4: <p align=\"center\">\nL5:   <a href=\"https://www.amd.com/en/developer/resources/technical-articles/2026/geak-v4.html\"><b>📝 Blog</b></a>\nL6:   &nbsp;•&nbsp;\nL7:   <a href=\"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/geak/en/latest/\"><b>📚 Documentation</b></a>\nL8: </p>\nL9: GEAK is an autonomous optimization agent that makes AMD GPUs run faster, automatically. Built as a\nL10: multi-agent system with an evolving knowledge base, it learns from every optimization run and continuously\nL11: improves its strategies over time. Point it at a single kernel or a live model-serving stack such as vLLM or\nL12: sglang, and GEAK runs the full optimization loop: it finds the bottlenecks, generates and tunes better kernels\nL13: across paths such as Triton, FlyDSL, TileLang, and HIP, and validates the speedup on the real system. What\nL14: normally takes weeks of expert kernel engineering becomes an automated, repeatable, and self-improving process.\nL15: GEAK targets AMD Instinct MI GPUs (CDNA, e.g. gfx942 / gfx950; the on-box card is auto-detected), and also runs\nL16: on RDNA3.5 client parts (gfx1151 / Radeon 8060S) and the validated RDNA4 product Radeon AI PRO R9700\nL17: (gfx1201). gfx1201 is an ISA shared by other products; it does not by itself select R9700 images,\nL18: calibrated peaks, or serving policy. It is driven by\nL19: Claude Code and orchestrated by deterministic JS Workflows. It ships two workflows, each for a different scenario:\nL20: | Workflow | Scope | What it optimizes |\nL21: | --- | --- | --- |\nL22: | [e2e_workflow](e2e_workflow/) | Whole-model serving | End-to-end sglang / vLLM throughput of a full LLM |\nL23: | [kernel_workflow](kernel_workflow/) | Single kernel | Latency / speedup of a single AMD GPU kernel (Triton, HIP, CK, FlyDSL, …) |\nL24: Use e2e_workflow to raise a whole model's serving throughput: it triages hot kernels, pulls the cheapest levers\nL25: first, and recursively calls kernel_workflow for the kernels worth fixing. Use kernel_workflow on its own to\nL26: optimize a single kernel.\nL27: \nL28: ---\nL29: ## Architecture\nL30: \nL31: <p align=\"center\">\nL32:   <img src=\"docs/assets/GEAK_v4_framework.png\" alt=\"GEAK v4 Optimization Pipeline\" width=\"900\">\nL33: </p>\nL34: \nL35: ---\nL36: \nL37: ## Getting started\nL38: ### 1. Prerequisites\nL39: \nL40: - An **AMD Instinct MI GPU** (CDNA, e.g. gfx942 / gfx950), an **RDNA3.5 part** (gfx1151), or a **Radeon AI PRO R9700** (validated RDNA4/gfx1201 product), **ROCm 6+**, a profiler (`rocprof-compute` /\nL41:   `rocprofv3` / `rocprof`; RDNA4 PMCs may be sparse — kernel-trace still counts), Python 3.8+.\nL42: - For E2E: a running-capable serving backend (`sglang`, `vllm`, or `atom`; R9700 is vLLM-only) and the model weights on disk.\nL43: > **⚠️ Build your kernel environment first.** GEAK does **not** install the toolchains your kernels\nL44: > need (e.g. PyTorch, Triton, FlyDSL, hipBLASLt) — these differ per kernel. Set up and verify the\nL45: > baseline builds/runs before starting a workflow.\nL46: ### 2. Set up\nL47: \nL48: Installing GEAK does three things: installs the `geak` Python package + deps, clones the GEAK repo, and installs\nL49: the Claude Code CLI. By default the repo lands in `./GEAK` under the directory you run the command from (override\nL50: the location with `GEAK_HOME`). Pick either method — both end up the same:\nL51: \nL52: **A. One-liner** — run it in the directory where you want GEAK to live:\nL53: \nL54: ```bash\nL55: pip install \"git+https://github.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK\"\nL56: ```\nL57: **B. Clone first** — if you'd rather have the checkout up front (e.g. to work on a branch):\nL58: \nL59: ```bash\nL60: git clone https://github.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK.git\nL61: cd GEAK\nL62: pip install .\nL63: ```\nL64: ### 3. Launch Claude Code in auto mode\nL65: \nL66: **Set up PATH and API access**\nL67: \nL68: You'll need to add `~/.local/bin` to your PATH and configure API access yourself — follow the installer's printed next-steps:\nL69: \nL70: ```bash\nL71: # Option 1: Anthropic API directly\nL72: export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...\nL73: # Option 2: Standard gateway (x-api-key / bearer)\nL74: export ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://your-gateway\nL75: export ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=your-token\nL76: ```\nL77: \nL78: > If your gateway authenticates with a **custom header** instead of `x-api-key` / a bearer token\nL79: > (e.g. `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key`), set it via `ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS`:\nL80: > ```bash\nL81: > export ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://your-gateway\nL82: > export ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS=\"Your-Header-Name: <your-key>\"\nL83: > ```\nL84: \nL85: **Launch Claude Code**\nL86: The workflows spawn many sub-agents and run profiling / benchmark / build commands on the box, so run\nL87: Claude Code with permissions auto-approved (≥ 2.1.177 for the dynamic Workflow feature):\nL88: \nL89: ```bash\nL90: IS_SANDBOX=1 claude --dangerously-skip-permissions\nL91: ```\nL92: \nL93: Then just describe what you want in natural language (examples below). Claude Code resolves the paths and\nL94: invokes the `Workflow` tool for you.\nL95: \nL96: ---\nL97: ## e2e_workflow — whole-model serving throughput ⭐\nL98: \nL99: `e2e_workflow/` raises the **sglang / vLLM serving throughput** of a whole LLM. It is a *system layer*\nL100: that wraps — and recursively calls — the single-kernel kernel_workflow:\nL101: 1. **Preflight** the env (GPU arch, backend, model).\nL102: 2. **Profile** a running server on your exact workload.\nL103: 3. **Triage** hot kernels by **Amdahl** (`pct_gpu_time × achievable_speedup`).\nL104: 4. **Pull levers cheapest-first** — config/backend sweep → head GEMM/attention bake-off (aiter per-shape\nL105:    tune + a kernel *authored* via the recursive kernel layer, FlyDSL-first for GEMM) → editable-kernel\nL106:    milestone loop.\nL107: 5. **Overlay** each accepted change back **reversibly**, gated on a measured warm-server throughput delta\nL108:    (interleaved A/B, 0.5% band + engagement proof + output parity).\nL109: Every run writes a complete **`final_report.md`** (with a Phases tree + artifacts tree).\nL110: ### Example\nL111: \nL112: ```\nL113: use path_to_GEAK/e2e_workflow to optimize inference for /models/Qwen3.5-27B-FP8, sglang, ISL/OSL=1024, conc=64, gpus 0,1,2,3\nL114: ```\nL115: \nL116: **Output** lands under `e2e_workflow/exp/e2e_<model>_<timestamp>/` — `final_report.md`,\nL117: `architect_report.md`, `final/` (overlay + patch + `final_launch.sh`), and per-stage artifacts.\nL118: ### Example — AgentX trace replay\nL119: \nL120: `ISL/OSL=…` above describes a synthetic sweep, where every request has the same shape. An AgentX\nL121: submission instead replays a recorded corpus: the client owns the request mix and the duration. A\nL122: standalone run is graded on **InferenceX P90 interactivity** (`1000 / P90(ITL)` in tok/s/user, the\nL123: x-axis of the InferenceX pareto), with total tok/s reported beside it as a guard. Say so, and the\nL124: workflow configures the whole run for it:\nL125: ```\nL126: use path_to_GEAK/e2e_workflow to optimize inference for /models/Kimi-K3 on vllm, gpus 0-7 tp=8,\nL127: workload_kind=agentx_trace_replay (the canonical AgentX corpus, graded on InferenceX P90 interactivity).\nL128: isl/osl 114000/819 are the average shape to OPTIMIZE for, not a benchmark to reproduce.\nL129: ```\nL130: \nL131: Check the box before starting — every AgentX prerequisite is otherwise invisible until a server has\nL132: already been launched and warmed:\nL133: \nL134: ```bash\nL135: MODEL=/models/Kimi-K3 bash e2e_workflow/scripts/agentx_smoke.sh\nL136: ```\nL137: See [`e2e_workflow/README.md` → *Standalone AgentX*](e2e_workflow/README.md#standalone-agentx-trace-replay-no-orchestrator)\nL138: for the full arg spelling, what the declaration changes, and the two things that surprise people\nL139: (`isl`/`osl` are not a benchmark; search legs are non-canonical by design).\nL140: \nL141: ---\nL142: ## kernel_workflow — single kernel\nL143: \nL144: `kernel_workflow/` optimizes a single AMD GPU kernel — Triton, HIP, CK, FlyDSL, or any AMD GPU source:\nL145: Director → TechLead → specialist engineers (algorithm / memory / compute / host_runtime), multi-round and\nL146: budget-controlled, with each patch independently verified before it's accepted.\nL147: ### Example\nL148: \nL149: ```\nL150: use path_to_GEAK/kernel_workflow to optimize path_to_GEAK/examples/tasks/knn\nL151: ```\nL152: \nL153: ```\nL154: use path_to_GEAK/kernel_workflow to optimize /path/to/silu, budget 8, focus on wrapper overhead\nL155: ```\nL156: \nL157: ### Batch (many kernels at once)\nL158: \nL159: Spawn one agent per kernel with isolated GPU assignments; GPU access is serialized via\nL160: `scripts/gpu_lock.sh` (flock-based), so kernels can safely share GPUs.\nL161: \nL162: ---\nL163: ## Why Workflows\nL164: \nL165: Control flow — the budget loop, fan-out, verification, and stop conditions — is **deterministic JS** in\nL166: `kernel_workflow.js` / `e2e_workflow.js`. LLM agents are called only for judgement (analysis, strategy,\nL167: optimization). This makes runs reliable and reproducible.\nL168: ## Results — single-kernel\nL169: \nL170: 12 HIP kernels, measured on AMD MI300X (gfx942) (excluding mla_decode; FAIL counted as 1.0x):\nL171: \nL172: | Method | LLM | Geo Mean |\nL173: | ------ | --- | -------- |\nL174: | GEAK_v3 (baseline) | Opus 4.8 | 1.90x |\nL175: | **kernel_workflow** | Opus 4.8 | **3.68x** |\nL176: ## Repository layout\nL177: ```\nL178: GEAK/\nL179: ├── e2e_workflow/        # ⭐ End-to-end LLM serving-throughput optimizer (wraps kernel_workflow/)\nL180: │   ├── e2e_workflow.js   # system-layer orchestration (config / head-GEMM / kernel tracks + e2e gate)\nL181: │   ├── roles/  knowledge/  scripts/   # adapters/{sglang,vllm,atom}.sh, op_bench.py, parse_profile.py, …\nL182: │   └── README.md / PLAN.md\nL183: ├── kernel_workflow/     # Single-kernel optimizer\nL184: │   ├── kernel_workflow.js       # deterministic JS orchestration\nL185: │   ├── roles/  knowledge/  scripts/   # gpu_lock.sh, profile_kernel.sh\nL186: │   └── README.md\nL187: ├── perf_knowledge/      # AMD operator × backend SOTA knowledge base (REFERENCE ONLY)\nL188: ├── kb_artifacts/        # machine-produced best patches (code-carrying, gitignored, warm-start source)\nL189: ├── examples/            # Example kernel tasks, benchmark comparisons, real e2e runs\nL190: └── exp/                 # Experiment outputs (timestamped per run)\nL191: ```\nL192: ## Running GEAK on the codex CLI\nL193: \nL194: GEAK also runs on the **codex CLI**, with the same `.js` workflows unmodified.\nL195: \nL196: ### 1. Install\nL197: \nL198: ```bash\nL199: node -v                            # need Node.js v20+\nL200: npm i -g @openai/codex@0.146.1     # pin 0.146.1 -- 0.147 breaks with gateways\nL201: codex --version                    # expect 0.146.1\nL202: node interface/runtime/engine/selftest.mjs  # optional: runtime checks, needs no GPU and no key\nL203: ```\nL204: ### 2. Configure\nL205: \nL206: One variable is the whole configuration: it **selects codex** *and* **configures its provider**.\nL207: \nL208: ```bash\nL209: export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...        # -> OpenAI official (api.openai.com)\nL210: # export GEAK_AMDKEY=<32hex>        # -> AMD gateway (llm-api.amd.com/Unified)\nL211: # export OPENAI_BASE_URL=...        # -> any other OpenAI-compatible endpoint; wins over both\nL212: ```\nL213: ### 3. Run\nL214: \nL215: Natural-language launch is **not wired up for codex yet**, so drive it from the command line —\nL216: `run_e2e.py` for a whole model, `run_workflow.mjs` for a single kernel:\nL217: ```bash\nL218: # e2e (whole-model serving throughput). A JSON says WHAT to optimize -- the same information the\nL219: # natural-language example above carries. Filename is yours -- it is just the first argument.\nL220: cat > run_spec.json <<'JSON'\nL221: { \"schema_version\": 2,\nL222:   \"model_path\": \"/models/Qwen3.5-27B-FP8\",\nL223:   \"framework\": \"sglang\", \"tp\": 1, \"gpu_ids\": \"0\",\nL224:   \"workload\": { \"isl\": 1024, \"osl\": 1024, \"conc\": 64 },\nL225:   \"exp_root\": \"/abs/work/geak\" }\nL226: JSON\nL227: # required: model_path, exp_root (basename MUST be `geak`); rest has defaults -- interface/run_e2e.md\nL228: python interface/run_e2e.py run_spec.json result.json    # auto-routes to the codex runtime\nL229: # single kernel:\nL230: node interface/runtime/engine/run_workflow.mjs kernel_workflow/kernel_workflow.js --agent codex \\\nL231:   --args '{\"kernel_path\":\"/abs/kernel\",\"workflow_dir\":\"'\"$PWD\"'/kernel_workflow\",\"budget\":6}'\nL232: ```\nL233: \nL234: Going further: [`interface/runtime/SETUP.md`](interface/runtime/SETUP.md),\nL235: [`interface/run_e2e.md`](interface/run_e2e.md).\nL236: ## Approaches compared\nL237: \nL238: How the workflows in this repo relate to the GEAK_v3 baseline:\nL239: |                        | GEAK v3 (baseline)                        | kernel_workflow                                                  | e2e_workflow                                                       |\nL240: | ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\nL241: | **Target**             | Single kernel                             | Single kernel                                                   | **Whole-model sglang/vLLM serving throughput**                    |\nL242: | **Agent backend**      | miniswe                                   | Claude                                                          | Claude                                                            |\nL243: | **Architecture**       | Orchestrator + parallel workers           | Hierarchical: Director → TechLead → Engineers → Merge           | e2e Director → System Architect → Profiler / Config Tuner / Kernel Extractor / e2e Integrator (wraps the kernel layer) |\nL244: | **Iteration**          | Multi-round                               | Multi-round, budget-controlled                                  | Multi-round, Amdahl-triaged, budget-controlled                    |\nL245: | **Orchestration**      | Python                                    | **Deterministic JS** — loop/parallelism/verification in code   | **Deterministic JS**                                              |\nL246: | **Verification**       | Orchestrator verifies                     | **Pipelined** — each patch verified by a separate agent        | **Warm-server interleaved A/B** — throughput delta + engagement proof + output parity |\nL247: | **Engineer types**     | Generic                                   | **Specialist**: algorithm, memory, compute, host_runtime       | System roles + the specialist kernel squad via the recursive layer |\nL248: | **Cross-round memory** | miniswe-memory control                    | **Explicit**: insight blackboard + hypothesis ledger           | **Explicit**: insight blackboard + per-backend knowledge          |\nL249: | **Best for**           | Programmatic kernel optimization          | Single-kernel gains with high reliability/reproducibility      | **Raising end-to-end serving throughput of a full model**         |\nL250: ## License\nL251: \nL252: GEAK is licensed under the Apache License 2.0 — see [LICENSE.md](LICENSE.md).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alibaba/atrex-kernel-agent/v0.3.0/README.md)\nciteturn40view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn33view1\",\"lineno\":1}); Total lines: 117\nL0: <div align=\"center\">\nL1: \nL2: <picture>\nL3:   <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"assets/aka-logo-light.png\">\nL4:   <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"assets/aka-logo-dark.png\">\nL5:   <img alt=\"Atrex Kernel Agent (AKA)\" src=\"assets/aka-logo-dark.png\" width=\"55%\">\nL6: </picture>\nL7: \nL8: **An orchestrated agent system for production-grade GPU kernel development**\nL9: \nL10: </div>\nL11: \nL12: ---\nL13: ## Overview\nL14: \nL15: Atrex Kernel Agent (AKA) turns an evaluator-owned operator into a measured, optimized GPU kernel.\nL16: It coordinates coding agents, GPU profiling, correctness checks, performance verification, Git\nL17: isolation, recovery, and final packaging while keeping acceptance and termination under mechanical\nL18: supervisor control.\nL19: The repository has one supported entry point, `orchestrator/optimize.py`. The internal\nL20: `long_horizon/` package supplies the episode engine; it is not a second CLI.\nL21: The supervisor establishes an evaluator-backed V0 and, when enabled, a framework-native V1. Coding\nL22: agents then explore fast, full, or goal episodes in isolated Git worktrees. The supervisor owns\nL23: correctness, production policy, performance verification, and promotion; every outcome is recorded\nL24: without letting an unverified candidate replace the incumbent.\nL25: ![Atrex Kernel Agent v0.3.0 architecture and workflow](assets/atrex-architecture-current.png)\nL26: \nL27: AKA supports:\nL28: - SOL-ExecBench and native Atrex-Bench operator layouts;\nL29: - NVIDIA, AMD, and T-Head PPU (zwm890p) targets through isolated sandbox execution;\nL30: - gateway and Bubblewrap-isolated OpenSSH GPU execution, with automatic environment recovery;\nL31: - Triton, CuteDSL, CUDA, FlyDSL, and TileLang campaigns;\nL32: - Claude, Qoder, Codex, and Pi coding-agent backends;\nL33: - leaderboard and fail-closed production modes;\nL34: - automatic local plugins and structured GPU Wiki retrieval with traceable attribution;\nL35: - production-mode numerical probes and mode-specific performance verification;\nL36: - resumable, Git-isolated optimization with canonical measurement history.\nL37: ## News\nL38: - [2026-09] We released **Atrex Kernel Agent v0.3.0** with resumable fast, full, and goal episodes; native Atrex-Bench and multi-framework production campaigns; Claude, Qoder, Codex, and Pi backends; isolated SSH GPU execution with recovery; PPU profiling; plugin-backed GPU Wiki retrieval; and production-mode supplemental numerical probes. See the [v0.3.0 release notes](docs/releases/v0.3.0.md).\nL39: - [2026-08] We slimmed down **Atrex Kernel Agent** by consolidating on a single orchestrated workflow and removing legacy paths and redundant context for a smaller context footprint and lower token usage.\nL40: - [2026-07] We helped **Qwen3.8** rank **No. 1** on the **SOL-ExecBench FlashInfer operator optimization leaderboard**.\nL41: - [2026-07] We released **Atrex Kernel Agent v0.2.0** with an orchestrated clean-session loop, native SOL-ExecBench operator workflow, Triton-to-Gluon conversion support, and a fuller NVIDIA profiling toolchain. [[Release](https://github.com/alibaba/atrex-kernel-agent/releases/tag/v0.2.0)]\nL42: - [2026-07] We released **the Atrex paper**: [Are LLM-Generated GPU Kernels Production-Ready? A Trace-Driven Benchmark and Optimization Agent](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14541).\nL43: - [2026-06] We released **Atrex Kernel Agent v0.1.0** as the initial open-source version, with the GPU Wiki knowledge base, profile-driven optimization workflow, profiling tools, and reference templates. [[Release](https://github.com/alibaba/atrex-kernel-agent/releases/tag/v0.1.0)]\nL44: ## Quick Start\nL45: \nL46: See the [Quick Start guide](docs/quickstart.md) for prerequisites and complete runnable examples of\nL47: the orchestrated optimization loop.\nL48: \nL49: Or start a coding agent such as Claude Code, Codex, or Qoder in this repository and ask it to\nL50: launch an AKA optimization task. We recommend the following prompt:\nL51: ```text\nL52: Use AKA's orchestrator/optimize.py to start one optimization task for atrex-bench/xx. Put the workspace under ~/aka-opt, set the platform to H20, use the local sandbox, use claude as the Agent CLI, set max-iters to 300, specify cuda as the framework, and run in production mode.\nL53: ```\nL54: ## Documentation\nL55: | Document | Contents |\nL56: | --- | --- |\nL57: | [Quick Start](docs/quickstart.md) | Setup, commands, campaign steps, configuration, and outputs |\nL58: | [Architecture Design](docs/design.md) | Components, authority boundaries, state machine, verification, and recovery |\nL59: | [GPU Wiki](gpu-wiki/README.md) | Structured hardware/kernel knowledge, queries, and trace mining |\nL60: | [Local plugins](docs/plugins.md) | Extend AKA tools and Skills through automatically discovered local plugins |\nL61: | [v0.3.0 release notes](docs/releases/v0.3.0.md) | New features, correctness and measurement changes, and upgrade guidance |\nL62: Run `python orchestrator/optimize.py --help` for the authoritative CLI interface and defaults.\nL63: For a GPU server reachable through OpenSSH, use a dedicated low-privilege account and pass\nL64: `--sandbox-ssh user@gpu-host --sandbox-ssh-gpu 0` with an explicit `--framework`. AKA keeps Agent,\nL65: Git, memory, and episode state local, transfers only\nL66: the sandbox allowlist to a fresh remote temporary directory, and runs it in a mandatory networkless\nL67: Bubblewrap namespace before copying back requested artifacts. Use `--sandbox-ssh-runtime-bind` for\nL68: read-only venv or toolchain trees outside the minimal system mounts. Runtime sources are checked\nL69: against sensitive host paths after remote symlink resolution, and only the assigned physical NVIDIA\nL70: GPU is visible; MIG execution fails closed until capability-node assignment is supported.\nL71: If the remote environment fails its GPU health probe, AKA stops, preserves the active worktree, and\nL72: starts a detached monitor that resumes the original command after the server recovers. See\nL73: [Quick Start: Isolated OpenSSH GPU host](docs/quickstart.md#isolated-openssh-gpu-host).\nL74: The generated `stop-recovery.sh` also stops a recovered optimizer and leaves automatic recovery\nL75: disabled until the generated `recover.sh` is run explicitly. The recovery monitor stays with the\nL76: restarted optimizer: an unexpected exit returns to health polling and automatic restart, while only a\nL77: durably clean zero exit completes recovery.\nL78: ## Acknowledgements\nL79: \nL80: AKA builds on and learns from many open-source projects, including:\nL81: - GPU kernel projects: [CUTLASS](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass),\nL82:   [cutex](https://github.com/deciding/cutex), [cuLA](https://github.com/inclusionAI/cuLA),\nL83:   [FlashAttention](https://github.com/Dao-AILab/flash-attention),\nL84:   [FlashInfer](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer),\nL85:   [FlyDSL](https://github.com/ROCm/FlyDSL), [Triton](https://github.com/triton-lang/triton),\nL86:   [DeepGEMM](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepGEMM),\nL87:   [LeetCUDA](https://github.com/xlite-dev/LeetCUDA),\nL88:   [FlashMLA](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/FlashMLA),\nL89:   [Composable Kernel](https://github.com/ROCm/composable_kernel),\nL90:   [cute-gemm](https://github.com/reed-lau/cute-gemm),\nL91:   [hpc-ops](https://github.com/Tencent/hpc-ops),\nL92:   [AIter](https://github.com/ROCm/aiter), [quack](https://github.com/Dao-AILab/quack), and\nL93:   [TileLang](https://github.com/tile-ai/tilelang).\nL94: - Knowledge and agent tooling: [KernelWiki](https://github.com/mit-han-lab/KernelWiki),\nL95:   [modern-gpu-programming-for-mlsys](https://github.com/mlc-ai/modern-gpu-programming-for-mlsys),\nL96:   [ncu-report-skill](https://github.com/mit-han-lab/ncu-report-skill),\nL97:   [humanize](https://github.com/PolyArch/humanize),\nL98:   [AKO4ALL](https://github.com/TongmingLAIC/AKO4ALL), and\nL99:   [KDA](https://github.com/mit-han-lab/kernel-design-agents).\nL100: ## Citation\nL101: \nL102: If AKA is useful in your work, please cite the [Atrex paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14541):\nL103: ```bibtex\nL104: @misc{atrex2026,\nL105:   title         = {Are LLM-Generated GPU Kernels Production-Ready? A Trace-Driven Benchmark and Optimization Agent},\nL106:   author        = {Lingyun Yang and Yuxiao Wang and Shenghao Liang and Linfeng Yang and Daocheng Ying and Chunbo You and Rui Zhang and Luping Wang and Yinghao Yu and Guodong Yang and Liping Zhang},\nL107:   year          = {2026},\nL108:   eprint        = {2607.14541},\nL109:   archivePrefix = {arXiv},\nL110:   primaryClass  = {cs.AI},\nL111:   url           = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14541}\nL112: }\nL113: ```\nL114: ## License\nL115: \nL116: Licensed under the [Apache License 2.0](LICENSE).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPTXBench: Evaluating and Adapting LLMs for GPU Kernel Optimization with Architecture-specific PTX (https://arxiv.org/html/2608.17379v3)\nciteturn40view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.17379v3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 585\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 PTXBench L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Benchmark Tasks and Controlled Context L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Multi-turn Evaluation Protocol L20:   4. cite